## 1. Exact source-level bridge and its limit

For a supplied actual loaded set `W`, the
[preceding stress-face candidate](target_c_accessible_stress_transversal_or_degenerate_face_2026-10-08.md)
defines `P_0` as the normalized source stresses with zero diameter
reaction on `W`. Its available diameter edges are
`E_0=H[B\W]`.

The new chain is:

    E_0 is contained in one actual diameter star
      -> P_0 is empty or one explicitly determined straight-chain stress
      -> a stress-killing deletion has at most s_Z+1 labels,
         all in W except possibly one unloaded internal chain vertex
      -> canonical strict full-source motion after deletion
      -> Level I only after every deleted label is restored.

The star hypothesis is not proved for a general exceptional edge set.
The cardinality-restoration arrow is also not proved. No grid, path
or KKT mass count is substituted for either missing premise.

The geometry below is a finite theorem for any triangle-free
unit-separated planar source of diameter `D>1`. It uses every source
point for the separation and diameter bounds; source isolates are
never dropped.

## 2. Positive contact support lies in its loaded hull

Let `s=(alpha,beta)` be any normalized nonnegative equilibrium stress
on actual unit contacts and actual diameter pairs. Write `B_s` for
the endpoints of positive diameter coefficients, and `C_s` for
the positive contact support.

Every `b in B_s` has a nonzero diameter reaction. An actual diameter
partner gives a strict supporting functional at `b`: for `q!=b`,

\[
(b-b')\cdot(q-b)\le-|q-b|^2/2<0.
\tag{DS1}
\]

Thus a positive combination of the incident diameter vectors cannot
vanish, and equilibrium gives at least one positive contact at `b`.

A nontrivial component of `C_s` with no positive diameter endpoint
would have only nonnegative contact forces. Pairing its equilibrium
with positions gives `sum alpha_e=0`, a contradiction. At every
vertex without a positive diameter reaction, equilibrium makes the
position a weighted average of its positive contact neighbors.
The finite maximum principle therefore gives

\[
\boxed{V(C_s)\subseteq\operatorname{conv}B_s.}
\tag{DS2}
\]

This is also the selected harmonic-trapping conclusion in canonical
[Section 64.3](../targets/target-c/64-extreme-circuit-transfer-audit.md).
No full-source rigidity or PSD hypothesis is needed here.

## 3. A star cannot carry two positive diameter edges

Suppose the positive diameter edges of `s` form a star with hub `a`
and leaves `b_1,...,b_k`. Write
`b_i=a+D u_i`, where `u_i` are unit vectors.
The full source diameter bound gives

\[
|u_i-u_j|\le1,
\qquad \angle(u_i,u_j)\le\pi/3.
\tag{DS3}
\]

All leaf directions lie in one arc of width at most `pi/3`. Indeed,
relative to one leaf they lie in `[-pi/3,pi/3]`; the two angular
extremes differ by at most `2pi/3<pi`, so their ordinary difference
is their smaller circular angle and is at most `pi/3`.

Assume `k>=2`. Let `b_-` and `b_+` be the distinct angular extreme
leaves. The segments `ab_-` and `ab_+` are exposed sides of
`conv B_s`, and no other member of `B_s` lies on either side.

At `b_-` the sole diameter reaction is parallel to `a-b_-`.
By DS2, all positive contact neighbors lie in one closed half-plane
bounded by the line `ab_-`. Dotting equilibrium with an inward
normal to that line forces every positive contact neighbor onto
the line, hence onto the segment `ab_-`.

There is therefore a unit contact from `b_-` directly toward `a`.
At an intermediate point of that segment there is no positive
diameter reaction. The same supporting-normal argument puts every
positive contact neighbor on the segment; one-dimensional equilibrium
then forces the next unit contact toward `a`. A path cannot stop at
an unloaded point. Finiteness and source separation force it to
reach `a`. Consequently

\[
D\in\mathbb Z,\qquad
a+j u_-\in A\quad(0\le j\le D).
\tag{DS4}
\]

Apply the same argument to `b_+`. In particular both `a+u_-` and
`a+u_+` are source points. Their distance is

\[
2\sin\!\left({\angle(u_-,u_+)\over2}\right)\le1.
\tag{DS5}
\]

A strict inequality violates source separation. Equality makes an
actual unit equilateral triangle with `a`. Both are excluded.
Thus:

\[
\boxed{\text{A triangle-free source stress supported in a diameter
star has exactly one positive diameter edge.}}
\tag{DS6}
\]

This excludes a two-edge shared-endpoint stress even when it is
not extreme. It makes no assertion against two disjoint diameter
edges or a three-edge diameter cycle.

## 4. The single-edge stress is unique and completely determined

If `beta` is supported on `ab` alone, normalization gives
`beta_ab=1`. DS2 puts the whole positive contact support on `ab`.
The preceding line argument forces `D=m in Z` and the unique
unit chain

\[
p_j=a+j(b-a)/m,\qquad 0\le j\le m.
\tag{DS7}
\]

At each internal point the two positive contact coefficients are
equal. Endpoint equilibrium determines their common value:

\[
\alpha_{p_jp_{j+1}}=m\quad(0\le j<m).
\tag{DS8}
\]

All other contact and diameter coefficients are zero. Conversely,
if the full source contains DS7 for an actual diameter edge,
DS8 with `beta_ab=1` is a normalized source stress.
The source can have additional actual rows; their coefficients in
this stress are zero.

The positive mixed support has `m+1` rows of rank `m`, so it is a
rigidity-matroid circuit. On its `m+1` vertices,

\[
\sigma_*=m-1=D-1.
\tag{DS9}
\]

Its scalar energy is
`m sum_j(z_{j+1}-z_j)^2-(z_m-z_0)^2>=0` by Cauchy.
This is the sharp one-edge case of the previous candidate's
circuit-defect inequality, not a full-source feasible flex.

## 5. Exact classification of the exceptional star face

Suppose `E_0` is empty or is contained in one actual diameter star.
By DS6, every stress in `P_0` must be a single-edge chain stress.
There can be at most one such chain among the edges of `E_0`:
two chains from the same hub again violate DS5.

Therefore:

\[
\boxed{
P_0=\varnothing\quad\text{or}\quad P_0=\{s_{\rm chain}\}.
}
\tag{DS10}
\]

The second case occurs exactly when an edge of `E_0` has the full
straight unit chain DS7 in the actual source. In particular `P_0`
is empty whenever `D` is not an integer. The nonempty face is one
vertex of the full normalized stress polytope.

No conclusion about a general forest, an odd cycle, several stars,
or an exceptional edge set with many components follows.

## 6. A located transversal with at most one unloaded exception

Now use the canonical C-II source, `D>=9`, with
`dim_aff P=s_Z` from Section 64. If `P_0` is empty, the preceding
candidate selects a deletion `U subset W` with `|U|<=s_Z+1`.

If `P_0={s_chain}`, choose any internal chain point `r`.
This is an actual unloaded contact-core vertex: it lies strictly
between two source points of an actual diameter pair, whereas every
actual diameter endpoint is an exposed source-hull vertex.
Define the nonnegative affine functional

\[
f_r(s)=\sum_{c\ni r}\alpha_c,\qquad F_r=P\cap\{f_r=0\}.
\tag{DS11}
\]

Since `f_r(s_chain)=2D>0`, `F_r` is a proper face and
`F_r intersect P_0` is empty. If `F_r` is empty, deleting `r`
alone kills every normalized stress. Otherwise
`dim_aff F_r<=s_Z-1`. Apply finite Helly on `F_r` to the
zero-diameter-reaction faces indexed by `W`. There is
`U subset W` with `|U|<=s_Z` and

\[
F_r\cap\bigcap_{b\in U}F_b=\varnothing.
\tag{DS12}
\]

Deleting `R=U union {r}` therefore kills every normalized source
stress. A reduced-source stress would extend by zero to the displayed
intersection; at deleted loaded vertices zero diameter reaction also
forces zero contact reaction by DS1.

Thus in both alternatives:

\[
\boxed{|R|\le s_Z+1,\qquad |R\setminus W|\le1.}
\tag{DS13}
\]

The optional exception is the supplied actual unloaded chain vertex.
Canonical [Audit.87](../audits/target_c_contact_opening_transversal_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md)
gives a strict contact-opening and diameter-shortening finite motion
of the retained full source. In the chain case the two diameter
endpoints lie outside `W` and remain retained, so nonemptiness is
automatic. In the empty-face case retained nonemptiness remains the
explicit finite hypothesis, automatic in the Type-II application.

The deletion has no extra label cost beyond `s_Z+1`. It still loses
cardinality. The unloaded exception is not asserted grid-accessible,
and no simultaneous refill is supplied.

The [general two-stage codimension candidate](target_c_almost_accessible_transversal_face_codimension_2026-10-08.md)
allows at most `b_0` unloaded exceptions for an arbitrary exceptional
edge set, using canonical contact-row independence and the loaded-only
stress-cycle theorem. The star geometry sharpens that bound to one
regardless of `b_0`.

## 7. Exact certificates and scope

The checker certifies:

- straight integer chains with an additional retained contact isolate;
- the sharp triangle obstruction for two extreme 60-degree arms,
  and separation failure below 60 degrees;
- triangle-free three-arm Y sources whose positive diameter support
  is a cycle, showing that DS6 cannot be extended to all connected
  diameter supports;
- two-stage Helly/codimension counts on abstract simplexes and joins
  of cubes with simplexes, including the one-preselected-face case.

The valid planar fixtures have exact equilibrium, PSD and rigidity
checks, complete actual contact/diameter/triangle censuses, and
explicit smaller-diameter same-cardinality square-grid competitors.
They are nonglobal and do not satisfy canonical mixed rigidity.
Abstract simplexes are not planar sources.

Sections 2–6 supply the analytic candidate proof. Computation proves
the listed finite certificates only. It neither extracts a universal
exceptional star nor restores deleted labels. Both canonical C-II
branches and Level I remain open.
